  "answer": "To ensure the best outcome for your report and presentation at this point (week 10), you should take the following steps:\n\n1. **Immediately meet with your professor** to explain the situation in detail, including the lack of contribution from Mathew, Seth, and Sebastian, the plagiarized work they submitted, and the negative impact on the report quality. Provide evidence such as peer review sheets, meeting notes, and examples of plagiarized content. Ask for guidance on how to proceed and whether any accommodations can be made (e.g., grading individual contributions, allowing extra time, or adjusting group composition).\n\n2. **Focus on improving the report quality** by having you, Amelia, and Brian take over the neglected sections. Redo or heavily edit the parts assigned to the non-contributing members to ensure accuracy, originality, and proper sourcing. Prioritize the sections that carry the most weight in the grading rubric.\n\n3. **Prepare a strong presentation** by ensuring that all group members who are willing and able to contribute are assigned clear roles. If Mathew, Seth, and Sebastian are not reliable, plan for you, Amelia, and Brian to cover their parts. Rehearse the presentation multiple times to ensure smooth delivery.\n\n4. **Document everything** for future reference, including all communications, work logs, and evidence of plagiarism. This will be useful if you need to escalate the issue to a department head or ombudsperson.\n\n5. **Consider submitting a formal complaint** if the professor is unresponsive or unhelpful, following the university's academic integrity and conflict resolution procedures.\n\nBy taking these steps, you can maximize the quality of your work and ensure that your own efforts are fairly recognized.",
